Yes, this one is also Reactor-based, and it already has some logging for non-WebClientResponseException inside isRetryable(). However, it still doesn't log each actual retry attempt with a retry counter, which is the main gap you're addressing.

I would modify this RetryConfig rather than adding another separate listener class.

Changes needed

Add:

import reactor.util.retry.Retry.RetrySignal;

Then update createRetry():

public Retry createRetry() {
    return Retry.backoff(
                    notificationDataProcessorProperties.getMaxRetries(),
                    Duration.ofSeconds(
                            notificationDataProcessorProperties.getInitialRetryDelay()))
            .maxBackoff(Duration.ofSeconds(
                    notificationDataProcessorProperties.getMaxBackoff()))
            .jitter(notificationDataProcessorProperties.getJitterFactor())
            .filter(this::isRetryable)
            .doBeforeRetry(this::logRetry)
            .onRetryExhaustedThrow((retryBackoffSpec, retrySignal) -> {
                logRetryExhausted(retrySignal);

                return new RuntimeException(
                        "Retries exhausted for the request",
                        retrySignal.failure());
            });
}

Keep isRetryable() focused on the decision

I would remove the logging from isRetryable():

public boolean isRetryable(Throwable throwable) {
    if (throwable instanceof WebClientResponseException ex) {
        int statusCode = ex.getStatusCode().value();

        return statusCode >= 500 && statusCode < 600;
    }

    return true;
}

The current logging:

log.error("Is this request retryable?")

doesn't tell us that a retry actually happened.

Add retry logging

private void logRetry(RetrySignal retrySignal) {

    Throwable failure = retrySignal.failure();

    LogBuilder logBuilder = new LogBuilder()
            .withMessage("Retrying Notification Resource API request")
            .withField(
                    "retryAttempt",
                    retrySignal.totalRetries() + 1)
            .withField(
                    "exceptionType",
                    failure.getClass().getSimpleName())
            .withField(
                    "exceptionMessage",
                    failure.getMessage());

    if (failure instanceof WebClientResponseException ex) {
        logBuilder.withField(
                "statusCode",
                ex.getStatusCode().value());
    }

    log.error(logBuilder.toString(), failure);
}

This gives you:

Retrying Notification Resource API request
retryAttempt=1
exceptionType=WebClientResponseException
exceptionMessage=503 Service Unavailable
statusCode=503

and then again for attempt 2, attempt 3, etc.

Add final exhaustion logging

private void logRetryExhausted(RetrySignal retrySignal) {

    Throwable failure = retrySignal.failure();

    LogBuilder logBuilder = new LogBuilder()
            .withMessage("Retries exhausted for Notification Resource API request")
            .withField(
                    "totalRetries",
                    retrySignal.totalRetries())
            .withField(
                    "exceptionType",
                    failure.getClass().getSimpleName())
            .withField(
                    "exceptionMessage",
                    failure.getMessage());

    if (failure instanceof WebClientResponseException ex) {
        logBuilder.withField(
                "statusCode",
                ex.getStatusCode().value());
    }

    log.error(logBuilder.toString(), failure);
}

Final flow

API call
   ↓
Exception
   ↓
isRetryable()
   ↓
true
   ↓
doBeforeRetry()
   ↓
Log exception + retryAttempt
   ↓
Retry
   ↓
Exception again
   ↓
doBeforeRetry()
   ↓
Log retryAttempt=2
   ↓
...
   ↓
Retry exhausted
   ↓
Log final exception

One additional thing I noticed

This class has:

handleErrorMap()

which already logs WebClientResponseException, but for the non-WebClient exception path it doesn't currently log the exception, it just returns:

return new RuntimeException(
    "Unexpected error occurred: " + throwable.getMessage());

Since doBeforeRetry() will now capture the exception and stack trace for every retry, that addresses the retry logging requirement. I would not add another log there immediately unless your testing shows that the final non-WebClient exception is missing from the logs.

So for this application, the clean change is:

Existing RetryConfig → add doBeforeRetry() + final exhaustion logging → remove retry-decision logging from isRetryable() → preserve all existing retry configuration values.